  "label": "Structure Guided Computational Protein Design",
  "related": [
    "reference-learning-6805",
    "docking-complexes-d43f",
    "language-model-5590"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "rna-topic-39cd",
  "summary": "Recent preprints report structure-derived energy and in silico objectives yielding measurable gains in enzyme activity and antibody affinity and stability.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational protein design that uses structural or energy-based objectives to select sequence changes. GDEE, an automated in silico active-site engineering method, is applied to FastPETase and ThermoStable-PETase and identifies S238Y, which boosts PET film degradation at 40 °C. LAffAb, an energy-guided approach, designs multi-mutation antibody libraries from structures and co-optimizes affinity, reported up to 30-fold, together with stability. What is new is the application of structure-derived energy or in silico objectives to enzyme and antibody variants, with affinity and stability optimized jointly in the antibody case. A third item, combining Minisci C-H alkylation HTE data, graph neural network reaction prediction, and structure-based scoring to reach subnanomolar MAGL inhibitors, is a small-molecule hit-to-lead workflow and sits adjacent to this thread rather than within it.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-30"
}
